      steps: [
        { id: 'hook', type: 'hook', art: 'nail', scene: 'Drop a clean iron nail into blue copper sulphate solution. After about 20 minutes, the nail has a reddish-brown coat and the blue colour has faded to pale green.', question: 'Where did the reddish-brown coat come from?' },
        { id: 'disp', type: 'explanation', title: 'A stronger metal takes the place of a weaker one', idea: 'In a displacement reaction, a more reactive element pushes a less reactive element out of its compound and takes its place.', example: 'Think of a game of musical chairs where a stronger player can push another off a chair. Iron is more reactive than copper, so it takes copper\'s place: Fe + CuSO4 -> FeSO4 + Cu. The pushed-out copper coats the nail.', steps: ['Iron sulphate is pale green: that explains the colour change.', 'Copper metal is reddish-brown: that is the coat on the nail.', 'Zinc does the same: Zn + CuSO4 -> ZnSO4 + Cu.', 'Lead does too: Pb + CuCl2 -> PbCl2 + Cu.'], terms: [{ en: 'displace', local: 'विस्थापित करना' }] },
        { id: 'lab', type: 'lab', title: 'Iron in copper sulphate', task: 'Run the reaction and watch both the nail and the solution.', presets: ['nail_copper'] },
        { id: 'order', type: 'explanation', title: 'Who can push whom', idea: 'A metal can displace only a metal that is less reactive than itself. If it is less reactive, nothing happens.', example: 'Put a copper wire in green iron sulphate solution: nothing happens, because copper is less reactive than iron. A short reactivity order to remember: K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al, Zn, Fe, Pb, (H), Cu, Hg, Ag, Au (most reactive first). You will study it fully in Chapter 3.', steps: ['Find both metals in the order.', 'If the free metal is higher than the metal in the salt, it displaces it.', 'If it is lower, there is no reaction.'] },
        { id: 'c1', type: 'check', items: [{ id: 't05-c1', q: 'A copper wire is placed in iron sulphate solution. What happens?', options: ['Iron is displaced and coats the wire', 'No reaction, because copper is less reactive than iron', 'The solution turns blue at once', 'Hydrogen gas is given off'], answer: 1, hint: 'Which is higher in the reactivity order, iron or copper?', why: 'Copper is below iron, so it cannot push iron out of its compound.', wrong: ['That would need copper to be more reactive than iron; it is not.', '', 'A blue colour would need copper sulphate to form, which needs a reaction.', 'No gas forms; there is no reaction.'] }] },
        { id: 'mis1', type: 'misconception', belief: 'Any metal put in any salt solution will react.', truth: 'Only a more reactive metal displaces a less reactive one.', why: 'Silver in copper sulphate, or copper in zinc sulphate, does nothing, because the free metal is weaker.' },
        { id: 'kp', type: 'key_points', points: ['Displacement: a more reactive element replaces a less reactive one in a compound.', 'Fe + CuSO4 -> FeSO4 + Cu: blue to pale green, brown coat on iron.', 'A less reactive metal cannot displace a more reactive one.'] },
        { id: 'tip', type: 'exam_tip', tip: 'When asked "what will you observe", give two observations: the change in the metal and the change in the colour of the solution.' },
        { id: 'pr', type: 'practice', items: [
          { id: 't05-p1', q: 'Zinc is added to copper sulphate solution. Which products form?', options: ['ZnO and CuO', 'ZnSO4 and Cu', 'Zn and CuSO4', 'No reaction'], answer: 1, hint: 'Zinc is more reactive than copper.', why: 'Zinc takes copper\'s place: Zn + CuSO4 -> ZnSO4 + Cu.', wrong: ['No oxygen reacts here; it is a displacement in solution.', '', 'Those are the starting substances, not the products.', 'Zinc is more reactive than copper, so it does react.'] },
          { id: 't05-p2', q: 'Which pair will react?', options: ['Copper + zinc sulphate', 'Silver + copper sulphate', 'Iron + copper sulphate', 'Copper + iron sulphate'], answer: 2, hint: 'The free metal must be above the metal in the salt.', why: 'Iron is above copper, so iron displaces copper from copper sulphate.', wrong: ['Copper is below zinc, so no reaction.', 'Silver is below copper, so no reaction.', '', 'Copper is below iron, so no reaction.'] },
          { id: 't05-p3', q: 'Why does the blue colour of copper sulphate fade when iron is added?', options: ['Copper sulphate evaporates', 'Copper sulphate is used up and pale green iron sulphate forms', 'Iron is blue', 'The water reacts with iron'], answer: 1, hint: 'The blue colour belongs to the copper compound.', why: 'As copper is displaced, blue copper sulphate is replaced by pale green iron sulphate.', wrong: ['Nothing evaporates at room temperature in 20 minutes.', '', 'Iron is grey.', 'The colour change is from the displacement, not from the water.'] },
        ] },
      ],
      check: { pass: 4, items: [
        { id: 't05-k1', ref: 'disp', q: 'Fe + CuSO4 -> FeSO4 + Cu is a:', options: ['combination reaction', 'decomposition reaction', 'displacement reaction', 'double displacement reaction'], answer: 2, hint: 'An element takes another element\'s place.', why: 'Iron, an element, replaces copper in its compound.', wrong: ['There is more than one product.', 'There is more than one reactant.', '', 'Double displacement swaps parts between two compounds; here one reactant is an element.'] },
        { id: 't05-k2', ref: 'disp', q: 'The reddish-brown coating on the iron nail is:', options: ['rust', 'copper', 'iron sulphate', 'copper oxide'], answer: 1, hint: 'Which metal was pushed out of the solution?', why: 'Displaced copper metal deposits on the nail; copper is reddish-brown.', wrong: ['Rust takes days in air and water; this forms in minutes in the solution.', '', 'Iron sulphate stays dissolved and makes the solution green.', 'No oxygen reacts here.'] },
        { id: 't05-k3', ref: 'order', q: 'Which metal can displace lead from lead chloride solution?', options: ['Copper', 'Silver', 'Zinc', 'Gold'], answer: 2, hint: 'Which one is above lead in the order?', why: 'Zinc is above lead, so it displaces it.', wrong: ['Copper is below lead.', 'Silver is below lead.', '', 'Gold is the least reactive of all.'] },
        { id: 't05-k4', ref: 'mis1', q: 'A silver spoon is left in copper sulphate solution. What happens?', options: ['Copper coats the spoon', 'Nothing happens', 'The spoon dissolves quickly', 'The solution turns green'], answer: 1, hint: 'Is silver more or less reactive than copper?', why: 'Silver is less reactive than copper, so it cannot displace it.', wrong: ['That would need silver to be more reactive than copper.', '', 'Silver does not react with copper sulphate.', 'Green would need iron sulphate, which needs iron.'] },
        { id: 't05-k5', ref: 'disp', q: 'In Pb + CuCl2 -> PbCl2 + Cu, which metal is more reactive?', options: ['Lead', 'Copper', 'Both equal', 'Cannot say'], answer: 0, hint: 'Which one ended up in the compound?', why: 'Lead pushed copper out, so lead is more reactive.', wrong: ['', 'Copper was the one pushed out, so it is less reactive.', 'If they were equal, there would be no displacement.', 'The reaction itself tells us: the one that displaces is more reactive.'] },
      ] },
